Validate and smooth ambient light readings before brightness follows them

Subject: [PATCH] adaptive-brightness: validate ALS input and confirm bucket changes

The ISHTP ambient light sensor on this machine delivers roughly 60% unusable
samples: dropped reports that read 0, negative counts, and saturation spikes
near 1e5 lux. Measured 2026-10-07; see docs/brightness.md in
xps-fedora-notes.

Upstream applies whatever arrives after a 1 s throttle, so a single corrupt
sample drives a full-range brightness jump. A better bucket curve cannot help,
because corrupt values fall outside every bucket and therefore always trigger
a switch.

Two changes:

  lib/SensorProxyService.js
    Validate each reading (floor, ceiling, rate of change), fold survivors into
    a sliding median window, and expose lastLuxValue as the smoothed value.
    Counters are available via the new stats getter.

  lib/BrightnessConfirm.js (new)
    Hold a bucket change until it has been sustained, with asymmetric timing:
    darkening confirms quickly to protect eyes, brightening slowly to avoid
    glare, plus a cooldown between changes. Kept free of GNOME imports so the
    policy can be tested directly.

  extension.js
    Confirm through BrightnessConfirm before applying a change, and read
    lastLuxValue rather than dbus.lightLevel on resume, wake and settings
    changes, which previously bypassed validation entirely.

Replay of a real 240 s capture: 119 samples, 38 accepted, 81 rejected;
brightness changes drop from 119 (range 15-100%, 32 steps over 2 points) to 2.

Note that GNOME Shell caches extension JavaScript for the life of the
process: after applying this, log out and log back in. Running
"gnome-extensions disable && enable" will leave the old code in place while
still reporting ACTIVE.

The fault was measured on kernel 7.2.8-300.fc45.x86_64. On
7.2.9-300.fc45.x86_64 the same machine reported 100% usable sensor samples
without this patch, so its real-world benefit here is unproven; it is kept as
insurance against the fault returning. See docs/brightness.md.

